{
  "result": "At dawn, the city pretends it has never been noisy. Delivery trucks whisper along wet streets, and windows brighten one by one, as if someone were lighting lanterns inside a paper model. The hour belongs to maintenance workers, bakers, dog walkers, and people carrying thoughts too large for sleep. They move without ceremony, each briefly visible beneath streetlamps before becoming anonymous again.\n\nI like this temporary anonymity. Daylight often asks us to explain ourselves: what we do, where we are going, why we chose one thing instead of another. Morning, before offices open, grants a gentler permission. A person can stand beside a closed caf\u00e9 holding a cup and simply be a person holding a cup.\n\nPerhaps that is why small rituals matter. Water boiling in a kettle, keys placed in the same bowl, a message sent to a friend without needing a reason: these acts build quiet bridges across uncertain days. They do not solve the world's enormous problems, but they remind us that attention is a kind of shelter. To notice steam curling above a mug is to admit that the present moment has texture.\n\nIt can be warm, bitter, fleeting, and entirely ordinary. Yet ordinary things, cared for deliberately, become evidence that life is more than its emergencies. The sun rises higher. Buses fill. Somewhere, a baker lifts fresh bread from an oven, and the whole street receives the news. Nobody applauds, but for a moment, everyone is fed by possibility. It feels like enough for now.",
